.TH CONTRACTOR 1 2026-11-06 "contractor 0.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
contractor \- contract repeated Lorentz indices in Mathematica ExpressionJSON
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B contractor
.RB [ \-threads
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-merge\-memory
.IR bytes ]
.RB [ \-cpuprofile
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-memprofile
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-blockprofile
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-mutexprofile
.IR file ]
.RI < input
.RI > output
.br
.B contractor \-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B contractor
reads one expression in Mathematica's ExpressionJSON form from standard
input, expands it, contracts every Lorentz index that occurs twice in a term,
folds the exact numeric coefficients, merges like terms, and writes the
result to standard output as one ExpressionJSON expression.
Its input follows FeynCalc's internal representation.
.PP
.B Plus
and
.B Times
are expanded, and so is
.B Power
with a positive integer exponent below 2^31; any other power is one opaque
factor.
.B Rational
and
.B Complex
of integers fold into the coefficient; any other, unless it divides by 0 or
holds a Pair, is read as the product or sum it stands for.
.B Pair
takes two arguments, each a
.BI LorentzIndex[ name ]
or a
.BI Momentum[ source ] ,
with an optional dimension label: none for 4 dimensions,
.B \(dqD\(dq
for
.IR D ,
or
.B [\(dqPlus\(dq,\ \-4,\ \(dqD\(dq]
for
.IR D \-4.
A momentum source that is a sum of labels with exact numeric coefficients is
read as one Pair per label.
Every other head is one opaque factor, written back as it came.
.PP
An index name that occurs twice in a term is contracted; a metric contracted
with itself gives 4,
.B \(dqD\(dq
or
.BR [\(dqPlus\(dq,\ \-4,\ \(dqD\(dq] .
One name in two dimensions contracts in the dimension both share, and 4 with
.IR D \-4
gives 0.
.PP
The input must be exactly one expression: a second value after it, JSON
objects,
.BR true ,
.BR false ,
.BR null ,
the empty array, nesting deeper than 9996 arrays, another dimension label and
a Pair whose two arguments differ in dimension are rejected.
.PP
The output is indented with tabs: 0 for an empty result, a single term, or a
.B Plus
of terms.
Its order comes from content alone, so it is the same bytes for any thread
count and any order of the input's summands.
It is written as it is encoded, so only exit status 0 means that standard
output holds the complete result.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-threads " n"
Run
.I n
workers, at least 1; the default is the number of CPUs.
The workers share every stage; parsing is done by one at a time.
.TP
.BI \-merge\-memory " bytes"
Hold about
.I bytes
of merged terms in memory, and move the rest to a temporary file, from which
they are merged again before the output is written.
The output is the same.
The default, 0, holds them all in memory.
.TP
.BI \-cpuprofile " file" "\fR, \fP\-memprofile " file "\fR, \fP\-blockprofile " file "\fR, \fP\-mutexprofile " file
Write a profile for
.BR "go tool pprof" .
Each file is created before the input is read and written after the run,
even when it fails.
.TP
.B \-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-h
Print the usage on standard error and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success: standard output holds the complete result.
.TP
.B 1
The input was rejected, leaving standard output empty, or reading, writing
or a temporary file failed.
A failure while writing the output leaves the part already written, and its
message begins
.BR "output incomplete" .
.TP
.B 2
A usage error: an unknown flag, a bad flag value, or an argument that is not
a flag.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TMPDIR
The directory of the temporary file of
.BR \-merge\-memory ,
named
.BR contractor\-* .
Where the system allows it, its name is removed as soon as it is made;
elsewhere the file is removed before the contractor exits.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Messages go to standard error and are ASCII: a
non-ASCII character quoted from the input is escaped, as in
.BR \eu03ba .
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
$ echo \(aq["Pair", ["LorentzIndex", "mu", "D"], ["LorentzIndex", "mu", "D"]]\(aq | contractor
"D"
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
The README and the
.I docs
directory of the source, at https://github.com/izokina/contractor:
.I docs/known-limitations.md
lists what input is rejected, and
.I docs/feyncalc-notation.md
reads the input in FeynCalc's notation.
